Looking at this change, the original `if/else` chain checks thresholds in descending order (PB → TB → GB → MB → KB), assigns the first matching unit, and falls back to `'B'`.

The replacement loop:
- builds the array `[PB, map.pb], [TB, map.tb], [GB, map.gb], [MB, map.mb], [KB, map.kb]` — same units paired with the same thresholds, in the same descending order;
- defaults `unit = 'B'` before the loop (matching the `else` branch);
- breaks on the first `mag >= threshold` match, which is exactly the `else if` short-circuit semantics;
- only executes inside the same guard (`!unit || !map[unit.toLowerCase()]`), so the `options.unit` fast path is untouched.

Tracing boundary values confirms equivalence: `mag = 0` → `'B'`, `mag = 1023` → `'B'`, `mag = 1024` → `'KB'`, `mag = 1 << 20` → `'MB'`, `mag >= map.pb` → `'PB'` in both versions. The only incidental differences (array allocation per call, hoisted `var i`/`var units`, which collide with nothing else in the function) are not observable through the API. I also verified the rest of the file matches upstream 3.1.2, so no other behavior was touched. This is an equivalent refactor.

